    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>Collagen is not a cellulite treatment, and no brand can honestly promise one.</strong> Two randomised trials, 219 women in total, have measured cellulite after an oral collagen supplement; both reported improvement, but each tested one specific branded peptide, the 2015 result held only in normal-weight women, and neither has been independently replicated.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Trial one:</strong> 105 women, 2.5g a day of a branded bioactive collagen peptide for six months. Cellulite degree and thigh waviness improved significantly, but only in the normal-weight group (P&lt;0.05).</li>
        <li><strong>Trial two:</strong> 114 women, 1,000mg a day of a low-molecular-weight peptide for 24 weeks. Cellulite severity, skin roughness and elasticity improved against placebo at weeks 12 and 24.</li>
        <li><strong>Why cellulite happens:</strong> it is the orientation of the fibrous septae under the skin, not the amount of fat. It affects an estimated 80 to 90% of women after puberty, at every body size.</li>
        <li><strong>The honest limitation:</strong> neither trial used the generic hydrolysed marine collagen in Collabloom, and a bigger milligram number is not the same evidence.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

