      <h2 id="what-is-cellulite">What is cellulite, and why do slim women get it too?</h2>
      <p>Cellulite is a change in the architecture of the tissue under the skin, not a measurement of how much fat you carry. That is why it appears on thighs and buttocks at every body size, and why losing weight does not reliably remove it.</p>
      <p>The skin is anchored to the muscle below by fibrous bands called septae. In men these tend to run across each other in a criss-cross pattern, with a thicker dermis above them. In women a greater share run straight down, perpendicular to the surface, so the fat lobules between them push up while the bands tether the skin down. That is the dimple.</p>
      <p>A comparison of 67 people reported in a 2020 review of cellulite pathophysiology put numbers on this: women with cellulite had a thicker dermis (P&lt;0.01), a thicker layer of subcutaneous fat (P=0.0001) and a higher proportion of perpendicular fibrous septae (P&lt;0.001) than men or than women without cellulite. The same review argues that changes in the dermis and the fat are not the primary cause, and that the treatments most likely to produce a lasting change are the ones that target the septae directly.</p>
      <p>Reviews put the prevalence at roughly 80 to 90% of women after puberty, while stating plainly that no precise epidemiological data exist. Cellulite is closer to a default feature of adult female skin than to a condition a minority has.</p>
      <p>This matters in an Indian consulting room because the standard advice a woman with dimpled thighs receives, often before anyone has looked at her, is to lose weight. If her septae run vertically and her BMI is already normal, that advice is aimed at the wrong tissue. The same mismatch between the advice given and the tissue involved runs through <a href="/pages/collagen-for-stretch-marks">stretch marks</a>, where the damage sits in the dermis rather than in the fat.</p>

      <h2 id="the-trials">Does collagen help cellulite? What the two trials found</h2>
      <p>Two randomised, placebo-controlled trials have ever used cellulite itself as an outcome after an oral collagen supplement. Both reported improvement. Both are small, both tested a specific branded peptide, and neither has been independently replicated, which is the part the supplement blogs leave out.</p>

