      <h3>The 2015 trial: 105 women, and a result that depended on body weight</h3>
      <p>Schunck and colleagues randomised 105 women aged 24 to 50 with moderate cellulite to 2.5g a day of bioactive collagen peptides or placebo for six months, and published it in the <em>Journal of Medicinal Food</em> in 2015. In the normal-weight participants, the degree of cellulite fell and thigh skin waviness reduced significantly against placebo (P&lt;0.05).</p>
      <p>The title of the paper is the caveat: the effect was body-mass-index dependent. In the overweight participants, the subcutaneous borderline shortened compared with their own baseline, but the change did not reach statistical significance against placebo. So the honest reading is one positive subgroup in one six-month study, not a result for everybody.</p>
      <p>Trade coverage at the time identified the tested ingredient as a specific branded peptide preparation from a German gelatin and collagen manufacturer. That is not a criticism of the trial, but it does mean the finding belongs to that material rather than to collagen in general.</p>

      <h3>The 2026 trial: 114 women, a smaller dose, a longer look</h3>
      <p>A second trial appeared in the <em>Journal of Medicinal Food</em> in 2026. Hwang and colleagues randomised 114 women aged 20 to 50 who had thigh cellulite and self-reported hair thinning to 1,000mg a day of a low-molecular-weight collagen peptide or placebo for 24 weeks. The supplemented group showed significant improvements against placebo in cellulite severity, the length of the dermal-subcutaneous border, skin roughness and skin elasticity at both week 12 and week 24. Hair shaft diameter also increased significantly by week 24.</p>
      <p>The paper includes pharmacokinetic work, reporting roughly 54-fold greater systemic exposure to the tripeptide glycine-proline-hydroxyproline over the first two hours compared with general collagen. That is a mechanistic argument for why a small dose of a very short peptide might do more than a large dose of a longer one — and it is also the reason the result cannot simply be read across to other products.</p>
      <p>Two further things should be said. The same low-molecular-weight peptide has been studied repeatedly in Korean skin trials, at least one of which was led by a researcher at the ingredient company that supplies it. And the funding and conflict-of-interest statement for the 2026 paper could not be read from this environment, so treat the sponsorship as unverified rather than as absent.</p>

      <h2 id="transfer">Why those results do not transfer to every collagen product</h2>
      <p>The dose on our own label is larger than the dose in either trial, and that is not an advantage we are entitled to claim. Collabloom contains 8,000mg of Japanese hydrolysed marine collagen per 30ml shot. The 2015 trial used 2.5g of one branded bioactive peptide. The 2026 trial used 1,000mg of one branded low-molecular-weight peptide. Neither tested generic hydrolysed marine collagen, and both papers argue that the specific peptide profile is what is doing the work.</p>
      <p>So a bigger milligram number is not better evidence. It is a different product. This is the same point the <a href="/pages/collagen-peptides">collagen peptides</a> article makes about molecular weight and absorption: the number on the front of the pack tells you about quantity, and the trials were run on identity.</p>
      <p>The trials also set the timescale: six months and 24 weeks. Nothing has been shown to happen to cellulite in four weeks, which is roughly how long most people give a supplement before deciding it failed. The realistic sequence of what collagen peptides move, and when, is in <a href="/pages/how-long-does-collagen-take-to-work">how long collagen takes to work</a>.</p>

