      <h2 id="what-works">What actually works for cellulite?</h2>
      <p>Honestly: nothing works well, and the best-evidenced options are clinic procedures rather than anything you can swallow or rub on.</p>
      <p>The benchmark review is still Luebberding, Krueger and Sadick in the <em>American Journal of Clinical Dermatology</em> in 2015. They analysed 67 studies, of which only 19 were randomised and placebo-controlled, and concluded that no clear evidence of good efficacy could be identified for any treatment evaluated. The only hints of benefit were acoustic wave therapy and the 1440nm Nd:YAG minimally invasive laser.</p>
      <p>A 2024 systematic review in <em>Aesthetic Plastic Surgery</em>, which describes itself as the first methodologically robust review of cellulite interventions, reached a similar conclusion a decade later: shockwave therapy, radiofrequency and subcutaneous injectables showed promising findings, with acoustic wave therapy, subcision and the 1440nm laser giving the most benefit. It judged study quality moderate and follow-up short.</p>
      <p>The drug story shows how hard the problem is. Collagenase clostridium histolyticum was approved in the United States in July 2020, the first injectable for cellulite, for moderate to severe buttock cellulite in adult women. The manufacturer stopped production in December 2022, citing bruising and possible prolonged skin discolouration. It was a commercial withdrawal, not a safety recall, but the outcome is the same: the only approved cellulite drug is no longer made.</p>
      <p>That leaves the topical aisle, where most money is spent. The review evidence is blunt: nothing applied to the surface reaches the septae, and no cream, oil, spray, massager or dry brush has shown a lasting reduction in dimpling in a properly controlled trial. A 2026 fact-check of a viral Indian anti-cellulite spray advertisement concluded the claim was false.</p>

      <h2 id="weight-exercise">Will losing weight or doing squats get rid of it?</h2>
      <p>Losing fat can make existing cellulite less visible. It does not remove it, and in some people it makes the skin look worse rather than better.</p>
      <p>The mechanism is straightforward. Less fat in the lobules means less pressure pushing up between the septae, so the surface looks smoother. But the septae themselves do not change, which is why cellulite persists in women who are lean. Sources also note the other side of this: where weight loss is large or rapid, the skin can sag, which is a different problem and one we have written about separately in <a href="/pages/collagen-for-loose-skin">collagen for loose skin after weight loss</a>.</p>
      <p>Resistance training is the usual recommendation, on the reasoning that more muscle under the skin fills the contour. We will not quote numbers for it: the figures circulating online, such as a 25 to 35% improvement in 12 weeks, could not be traced to any primary study in this research. Train for your bones, your knees and your metabolic health. Do not train on the promise of smooth thighs.</p>

