      <h2 id="see-a-doctor">When dimpled skin is worth showing a doctor</h2>
      <p>Ordinary cellulite is not a medical problem and needs no investigation. Some things that look like it do.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Pain or tenderness in the dimpled area.</strong> Cellulite does not hurt. Painful, tender fat, usually on the legs and often symmetrical, needs an examination.</li>
        <li><strong>Firm lumps or nodules under the skin</strong>, particularly if they are warm or the skin over them is red.</li>
        <li><strong>Swelling that pits</strong> when you press it with a finger, or swelling of one limb only.</li>
        <li><strong>Sudden onset.</strong> Cellulite develops over years. Dimpling that appears over weeks is something else.</li>
        <li><strong>Any dimpling or puckering on the breast</strong>, especially over a lump or where the skin seems tethered. This has nothing to do with cellulite and should be seen promptly.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>None of this is common. It is listed because "it is just cellulite" is a conclusion worth reaching after a look rather than before one.</p>

